  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the line $y=2x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{\u2032}(-1,1)$ and ${B}^{\u2032}(0,5).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{\u2032}$ and ${B}^{\u2032}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{5-1}{0+1}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 4x-y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
